<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21924v1/visual_compare.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/230;" width="476" height="230" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Qualitative comparison of 3DVG results. Incorrectly predicted 3D bounding boxes are highlighted in <span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>, while correct ones are in <span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E600;">green</span>. Key spatial and semantic cues are <span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of 3D holistic view selection (HVS).</span>
As mentioned in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 3D holistic view selection ‣ 3 Method ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, our SPAZER first holistically renders multiple perspective views and identifies the optimal one for the query text. To analyze the effectiveness of such design, we compare the following two variants: <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) BEV</span>: Skip view selection and use the BEV by default, which is also consistent with prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Random</span>: Use a random view instead of agent-selected one. In Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Qualitative comparison ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, BEV performs poorly as it may fail to reliably capture the target object. Random performs slightly better than BEV. In contrast, our agent leverages spatial understanding to select more informative views for subsequent steps, leading to improved overall accuracy (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. Random and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. BEV in average).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of candidate object screening.</span>
At the candidate screening stage (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Candidate object screening ‣ 3 Method ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), irrelevant object anchors are filtered out. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Qualitative comparison ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, incorporating our proposed Retrieval-augmented anchor filtering (RAF) on top of the text-based baseline consistently improves accuracy by <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\%</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because text-based matching is vulnerable to inaccurate object class prediction, whereas our RAF introduces the visual object table as a complementary cue, leading to improved robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of 3D-2D joint decision-making (JDM).</span>
The JDM module in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 3D-2D joint decision-making ‣ 3 Method ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> serves as the core of our approach, as it enables the integration of 3D spatial reasoning and 2D semantic verification within our agent.
To enable a comprehensive analysis, in Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Qualitative comparison ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we further include comparisons between w/o JDM and w/ JDM under each of the previously defined ablation settings.
Here, w/o JDM refers to our agent making decisions using <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D-only</span> input, which is same as previous 3D-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. In this setting, our method achieves an overall accuracy of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{52.4}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">52.4</mtext><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{52.4}\%</annotation></semantics></math>, outperforming ZSVG3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> and SeeGround <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> by <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
With the introduction of informative camera views as complementary 2D semantic context through camera view mapping, our agent achieves a substantial improvement—an average accuracy gain of <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">11.6%</span>. This result strongly supports the effectiveness of our spatial-semantic joint reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:61%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21924v1/topk_nview_.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:295/113;" width="295" height="113"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Ablation studies on parameter <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
In the candidate screening stage, our agent gives the Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most possible target object IDs. We evaluate the Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy (<span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, whether the ground-truth object is in the <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> candidates) and the overall grounding accuracy under different values of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (a) As <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy steadily improves, indicating a higher likelihood of including the ground-truth object into candidates. This also contributes to the improvement of overall accuracy. However, increasing <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a performance drop, suggesting that an excessively large <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> may introduce distracting candidates for subsequent decision-making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of rendered views <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
In Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (b), we show how varying the number of rendered views <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> affects the accuracy. When <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we only use the BEV by default.
As <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases from <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, the accuracy gradually improves. This is because more global views are provided for the agent to select from, allowing better observation of the target object.
However, as <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> further increases from <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, the accuracy starts to decline due to increasing overlap between views, which introduces redundant information and adds unnecessary noise, ultimately impairing the agent’s decision-making.
The Top-<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy follows a similar trend to the overall accuracy, albeit with smoother variations. This suggests that even when the target object is included in the candidates, suboptimal 3D global view can still hinder the subsequent 3D-2D joint reasoning, highlighting the importance of view selection.</p>
</div>
